;; A SESSION HANDOFF's DECISION CORE — moving an authentication that already
;; succeeded from the agent that completed it into the one that asked for it.
;;
;; Not to be confused with `handoff_core.kotoba`, which is one Bot giving work
;; to another. This file has nothing to do with Bots. The word is the same
;; because the shape is: something crosses a boundary, and the whole question
;; is what it must not carry with it.
;;
;; ## Why a session has to cross a boundary at all
;;
;; The native window is a webview with its OWN cookie jar, and RFC 8252 says
;; the authorization request must not open inside it. So the person signs in
;; in the system browser, the loopback callback lands there, and the session
;; cookie is set THERE — in a jar the native window cannot read. The native
;; window started the flow and ends up with nothing to show for it. That is
;; the blank screen, stated precisely.
;;
;; The fix is a second, separate secret: the starter keeps a claim token that
;; never enters a URL, never reaches the authorization server, and never
;; appears in a redirect. When the callback completes, the starter presents it
;; and receives a session of its own.
;;
;; ## Why the claim token is not the OAuth `state`
;;
;; Because `state` is published. It travels in the authorization URL, sits in
;; the address bar, lands in the authorization server's logs, and comes back
;; in a redirect. Anything that can be read by a bystander is not a bearer
;; secret, and a claim endpoint keyed on `state` would hand a session to
;; whoever read one. The claim token is generated in the same breath and goes
;; only into the JSON response the starter already receives.
;;
;; ## What this file refuses, and the failure each refusal is for
;;
;;   - not `origin-trusted` — a page on the open internet POSTing a guessed
;;     token at a loopback port. The HTTP layer already applies the same test;
;;     it is restated here for the reason `handoff_core/may-approve?` restates
;;     `bot_core`'s: a refusal is only reliable where the argument is made.
;;   - not `ready` — claiming before anybody authenticated. Without this, a
;;     claim token is a session that exists as soon as it is issued, and
;;     starting a sign-in would be the same as finishing one.
;;   - `claimed` — replay. A claim is a one-shot: the window that asked for
;;     the session got it, and a second presentation of the same token is
;;     somebody else holding a copy.
;;   - `expired` — a token that outlives its purpose. The gap between the
;;     callback landing and the window that is already polling picking it up
;;     is seconds. Anything longer is a session waiting to be stolen.
;;
;; A caller that is refused is told nothing about WHICH of the four it was.
;; The host collapses every negative answer into the same "not ready", so a
;; guessed token cannot be confirmed by the shape of its rejection. That
;; collapse belongs to the host: this file keeps the distinctions because
;; refusing precisely and answering vaguely are different jobs.
;;
;; ## Why it is written this narrowly
;;
;; Same native word-typed slice as the other cores: `:bool` scalars, a sealed
;; all-scalar record, `if`. Facts arrive as a record because a bare `:bool`
;; parameter is a backend gap (compiler ADR-0219).
;;
;; Parity: test/cloud/itonami/app/session_handoff_kotoba_parity_test.clj

(ns session-handoff-core
  (:schemas {;; What the host has established about one presented claim token.
             ;;
             ;; Deliberately absent: the token itself, the user it would sign
             ;; in as, the identity provider, and the authentication strength.
             ;; The first would put a live secret into a decision; the rest
             ;; are the CALLBACK's judgement, already made and already
             ;; recorded, and re-deciding them here would mean this file could
             ;; disagree with the authentication it is only supposed to move.
             ;;
             ;; `ready` is the host's answer to "did the callback complete for
             ;; this claim", not "does this claim exist" — a claim is written
             ;; when the flow starts, so existence proves only that somebody
             ;; started something.
             :session-handoff/claim
             [:record :session-handoff/claim
              [[:origin-trusted :bool]
               [:ready :bool]
               [:claimed :bool]
               [:expired :bool]]]})
  (:export [claimable? main]))

(defn claimable? [c [:ref :session-handoff/claim]] :bool
  ;; Origin first and alone, because it is the only one of the four that is
  ;; about WHO is asking rather than about the state of the claim. No
  ;; combination of ready, unclaimed and unexpired reaches past it.
  ;;
  ;; Then ready, then the two ways a ready claim is spent — used, or too old.
  ;; Nested `if` rather than one conjunction so each refusal is a separate
  ;; line to put a breakpoint on and a separate case in the parity corpus.
  (if (record-get c :origin-trusted)
    (if (record-get c :ready)
      (if (record-get c :claimed)
        false
        (not (record-get c :expired)))
      false)
    false))

(defn main [] :i64
  ;; Sentinel for the wasm host contract, not a battery. The assertions live
  ;; in the parity test where they can be compared against the cljc.
  0)
